  "text": "Here's the completed function `remove_adjacent_duplicates`, along with the provided unit tests.\n\nThis function removes all consecutive duplicates from the input list, keeping only the first occurrence of each sequence of repeated elements.\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n    \n    result = [lst[0]]  # Start with the first element\n    for num in lst[1:]:\n        if num != result[-1]:\n            result.append(num)\n    return result\n\nclass TestRemoveAdjacentDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_basic_functionality(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]), [1, 2, 3, 4])\n    \n    def test_repeated_elements(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7]), [5, 6, 7])\n    \n    def test_multiple_duplicates(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\n### How it works:\n- The function starts with the first element in the result list.\n- It then iterates over the rest of the input list, checking if the next number is different from the last one in the result list.\n- If it is different, it is added to the result list.\n- This effectively removes all adjacent duplicates, leaving only one occurrence of each consecutive group of identical elements.\n\n### Example:\nInput: `[1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]`  \nOutput: `[1, 2, 3, 4]`\n\nYou can run this by saving it as a `.py` file and executing it, or by running it in an interactive Python environment.",
